  "query_preview": "..The conversation of overnight activity has come to\nfruition, as showers and thunderstorms propagate northward through\nYavapai County. Storms are moving quickly and there doesn't appear\nto be much in the way of training thunderstorms, so we aren't\noverly concerned with any flash flooding at the moment. For now, \nthe main impact looks to be a few folks being woken up by some \nthunder. However, we do have a Flash Flood Watch in effect \nstarting at midnight and continuing through 5 AM MST Thursday. \n\nModel guidance hasn't been our best friend the last several days,\nbut from what I've seen recently, all models seem to agree today\n(Wednesday) will be the most active in terms of showers and \nthunderstorms. Where models quickly disagree is the timing. Some \nmodels suggests the worst of it will be overnight tonight/early \nWednesday morning, others suggest Wednesday afternoon/evening \ngetting the bulk of the activity. With how radar is looking right \nnow, I'd put more weight on the solutions suggesting this \nafternoon/evening being the busiest. WPC also has much of Arizona \nin a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall and also a section of the \nstate (Tucson to Payson roughly) in a Moderate Risk. So there is \nconfidence in continuous showers and thunderstorms throughout the \nday, with our southern zones likely having the greatest coverage.\nWhile any location will be susceptible to flash flooding, recent\nburn scars will be even more prone to flooding concerns. With \nthat being said, be sure to have a way to receive warnings and \nnever drive into a flooded roadway!\n\nThis active pattern is due to a large area of low pressure\npositioned across the Pacific Northwest. This pattern will shift\nby the end of the week and over the weekend as the low lifts more\ninland and a high pressure system moves towards the West Coast.\nThis will bring drier conditions across the state Saturday and\nSunday, but this drier trend looks to be short-lived with another\ndisturbance potentially moving in early next week. Stay tuned for\nmore details.",
